  "client_view_text": " In this way, a write-ahead log can prevent the loss of data that would be lost in the event of a crash.\nWhen a database updates the data, it first writes the changes to the write-ahead log. The write-ahead log is a record of all the changes that have been made to the data, in the order they were made. This means that even if the database crashes before it has a chance to update the data in memory, the write-ahead log still contains a record of the changes that need to be made. After the crash, the database can recover by re-applying the changes recorded in the write-ahead log to the data. This ensures that the data is consistent and up-to-date, even if the database crashed.\nThe write-ahead log serves as a safety net, allowing the database to recover from a crash by re-applying the changes that were recorded in the log. This means that even if the database crashes and some of the changes are not yet written to disk, the write-ahead log ensures that those changes are still applied to the data, preventing the loss of data. By using a write-ahead log, a database can provide a high level of data integrity and reliability, even in the event of a crash.\nThe write-ahead log can also be used"
